Minister of Commerce: Market promotion project for small-scale agriculture to help expand agricultural products to market
(Kampot): The Minister of Commerce, Mr. Pan Sosak, expressed his satisfaction with the project to promote the market for small-scale agriculture (AIMS) because it has been contributing significantly to the expansion of farmers' agricultural products to Marketing. The Minister encourages and encourages stakeholders to increase the effectiveness of the implementation of this project.
Expressions of satisfaction with the Small-Scale Agricultural Market Promotion Project were made during the Minister's visit to a rice farm in Boeung Nimol Commune, Chhuk District, Kampot Province. The visit was conducted to learn about public-private sector investment in the value chain of chickens and the link between agribusiness and marketing through market promotion projects for small-scale agriculture.
The visit was also attended by Mr. Chiao Tai, Governor of Kampot Province, as well as officials of the Ministry of Commerce and other departments in Kampot.
After visiting the farm and listening to the farm owners present the value chain of chickens in the farm, Minister Pan Sosak praised the efforts of the farm owners who have established this large farm. To produce and supply demand for farm chickens both in Kampot and outside the province.
The Minister emphasized that in order to make the investment between the public and private sectors on the chicken chain price chain more efficient, the "M Project" plays an important role and contribution. In particular, private companies and stakeholders in the sector must work together to make the chicken chain value chain work for the benefit of all stakeholders in the value chain, including small-scale producers.
Seeing the potential to expand the production and marketing of farmers of AM Project, we decided to choose 3PLE FARMS for today's visit to learn about the process of the company's chicken meat production value chain. And lead to any form of cooperation with the company in the spirit of partnership, investment in the public and private sectors.
Taking the opportunity, the Minister requested that the company integrate the AM project farmers into the company's existing potential market through the company's strategic marketing plan to increase the income of smallholder farmers living in the communities near the company and open the market. More farm chickens to improve the lives of smallholder farmers in Cambodia.
The minister also said that the AM project would facilitate the company in seeking funding to expand its business. In particular, the Ministry of Commerce will lead the implementation of the AM project and liaise with the company to participate in the One Village, One Product Fair and a campaign to buy Cambodian products to further expand the agricultural market.
Mr. Chan Sokty said that the AM project operates in 18 provinces and supports the value chain of five products such as vegetables, chicken, rice, cassava and raw silk. The project's direct target beneficiaries are smallholder farmers, including 75,000 poor and near-poor farmers, who volunteer to collaborate on project activities by participating in value chains.
The next target groups include agricultural communities, farmers' organizations, small and medium enterprises, agribusiness, service enterprises and industrial associations.
