Castro was one of the heroes who gave the Cuban people renewed hope by defeating dictator Batista in 1959. After becoming the leader of Castro, he also helped to develop the country better than Bastista by pursuing a policy of neutrality, non-alignment, and having the right plan to lead the country through political, economic and social revolution.
Fidel Castro was born on August 13, 1926, in a small village in Orion, Cuba, to a wealthy Spanish immigrant family. He was an anti-government leftist and followed the Marxist-Leninist ideology while studying law at the University of Havana, a prestigious university in the Cuban capital.
He was arrested and sentenced to 30 years in prison by Batista authorities. However, after a year in prison, he was pardoned.
In 1959, Castro returned to Cuba with only seven men and seven weapons. However, he persuaded the people to join the plan to overthrow the Bastista dictatorship until its success that same year.
After playing a major role in overthrowing the Bastille administration, Castro took over all of Cuba's military and political power as prime minister. As a member of the Soviet bloc, Castro was often harassed by the United States and sought to overthrow his regime, and the United States cut off diplomatic relations with Cuba. .
Castro reformed Cuba into a socialist state under the control of a single Communist Party and adhered to Marxist-Leninist theories. It should be noted that through such reforms, only Cuba was the first to do so, counting only the countries in the western hemisphere.
The formulation of economic policies, the health sector and the education sector are under the strict control of the state. In foreign affairs, Castro supported revolutionary groups opposed to imperialism, and he also supported the establishment of a Marxist-Leninist regime in Chile by sending troops to help the Allies, as well as sending medical teams abroad. Well.
In 2006, Castro handed over the reins to his brother, who became vice president to take over the government instead. It should be noted that Raul Castro, Fidel Castro's younger brother, was elected President by the National Assembly in 2008, two years after he took office.
As the longest-serving head of state in the 20th and 21st centuries, from 1959 to 2008, Castro was opposed by people around the world. But a large part of his people and his supporters regarded him as a champion of socialism and against imperialism, as he was a man who contributed to economic growth and good social justice, and in particular guaranteed independence. Of Cuba from the hegemony of the United States.
Critics see him as a dictator who violates human rights and undermines the economy. However, the struggles in Castro's life have been rewarded with many international awards and have had a profound effect on individuals and groups around the world.
Fidel Castro died at the age of 90 on November 25, 2016. Spokesman for the General Commission: Allegations that Cambodia has banned the right to conduct religious ceremonies is a baseless analysis with clear political tendencies
(Phnom Penh): Lt. Gen. Chhay Kim Khoeun, Deputy Commissioner General and Spokesperson of the National Police General Commission, considered the broadcast accusing Cambodia of suppressing the right to freedom of religion as a baseless analysis and political tendencies. .
The response of the spokesperson of the General Commission of the National Police came after these days, foreign radio stations that speak Khmer and some social media sites provided comments or analysis related to the allegations and the assessment of The Cambodian authorities say that they are suppressing and blocking the people's right to freedom of assembly and celebration of various religions.
Lt. Gen. Chhay Kim Khoeun confirmed to Fresh News: "We see this kind of analysis as an analysis from a pessimistic, dark and destructive environment. "It confuses the public and the Cambodian people."
The lieutenant general said that the above allegations are baseless, not based on the legal aspect and not on the actual situation, for three reasons:
Regarding the accusation that the right to hold religious ceremonies was banned, the spokesman of the National Police General Commission claimed that there was no law forbidding the organization of religious ceremonies, and he explained the following three points:
* First: In a context where the world is paying attention to prevention against Kovid 19 virus, Cambodia is also a country that suffers or strives to prevent it to protect the health and lives of its people. Therefore, health measures are necessary and must be implemented in accordance with the principles of social disparities. Therefore, large-scale gatherings are prohibited.
* Second: According to the Khmer tradition, the festival is usually held in a pagoda or in the house of the festival owner, and it is never organized in the market, shops and public streets, or in places other than The owner of the house of the original owner of the festival.
* Thirdly, if the ceremony is to be held with the permission of the local authority, it means that if you want to do it in a public place other than the pagoda other than the house of the funeral director, it is necessary to ask for permission from the authority. Public order. In addition, permission must be sought from the owner of the target location if the site is owned or privately owned.
