(Phnom Penh): The Board of Priests of the Kingdom of Cambodia has requested the Ministry of Cults and Religions as well as the relevant authorities to take legal action against the owner of the Facebook account "Troll Yim Ton" on the grounds that the owner of this Facebook account has trampled. Values of Buddhism and Khmer monks.
In a statement on Wednesday, July 15, 2020, the Board of Trustees pointed out that the Facebook account "Troll Yim Ton Ban" posted a video clip posted on social media as a song mixed with a cut image of a Buddhist monk. Pornography with women, using words in a deliberate manner, destroys honor, dignity, as well as the values of Buddhism, the state religion.
The Board of Priests considers this act to be a serious insult to Theravada monks in Cambodia, as well as to the Buddhists, who are deeply saddened by this unfortunate and degrading act.
The Board of Priests emphasized in the statement that the photos of the individuals taken by the above Facebook account with women were images that had been dealt with disciplined by the clergy at all levels, some of which were images of foreign monks and nuns. Some of the images of innocent priests were also attached to ridicule and humiliate the song in a harsh and unjust manner.
The Board of Priests noted that the "Troll Yimton" Facebook account had committed the same tragedy many years ago, taking on the role of a priest as a fully-intentioned professional in undermining the honor and dignity of God. Monks, as well as the values of Theravada Buddhism in Cambodia, which the Board of Priests of Cambodia cannot accept.
The Board of Directors strongly denies and condemns the dirty, immoral, corrupt and inhumane acts and requests the Ministry of Cults and Religions and the relevant authorities to take action against the above Facebook account administrator in accordance with legal procedures.
On the same day, the Ministry of Cults and Religions submitted a request to Samdech Krala Hom Sar Kheng, Deputy Prime Minister and Minister of Interior, and Mr. Khieu Kanharith, Minister of Information, to take action against the Facebook account holder.
