(Phnom Penh): On Tuesday morning, July 14, 2020, Samdech Techo Hun Sen, Prime Minister of Cambodia, paid a visit to the Fish Breeding Research and Development Center as well as a meeting with farmers in Peam Ro District, Prey Veng Province.
Samdech Techo Hun Sen, the Prime Minister of the peasant families along the Mekong River, is one of the leaders with the highest interest in agriculture. The head of the Royal Government of Cambodia is also the one who led Cambodia from a country without food to become a country with millions of tons of rice left today.
At a time when Cambodia, as well as the world, is facing the Kovid crisis 19, the agricultural sector is seen by the Royal Government as an important opportunity to support the economy, solve people's unemployment and help solve the difficulties in people's lives. Also. Samdech Techo Hun Sen urged the relevant ministries as well as the people of Cambodia to turn the problem caused by Kovid 19 into an opportunity for reform and development of the agricultural sector in Cambodia.
A meeting with farmers and a visit to the Fish Breeding Research and Development Center in Bati Village, Peam Ro Commune, Peam Ro District, Prey Veng Province, nearly 100 km from Phnom Penh, are considered as another contribution to the promotion of agriculture in Cambodia. . Especially during the crisis caused by Kovid 19.
In an interview with Fresh News on July 13, 2020, Mr. Veng Sakhon, Minister of Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries, summoned Samdech Techo to Prey Veng province as a clear reflection of the celebration of "Fish Day". July 1 and Plant Day July 9 ", which Cambodia did not celebrate this year because of Kovid 19.
Mr. Veng Sakhon especially saw that Samdech Techo's visit and talk with farmers at this time is an activity to inspire all relevant authorities, especially the sub-national administration and farmers to cultivate food crops in accordance with the principles of development. Green, animal husbandry and aquatic culture were active in the era of the Fourth Industrial Revolution, in which Cambodia was developing from a country dependent on agriculture to agro-industry.
Mr. Veng Sakhon also shared the history of the Freshwater Aquaculture Research and Development Center. Mr. Veng Sakhon said that the center was established in 1988 under the name "Fish Breeding Station". The center was established by the Fisheries Administration in collaboration with PADIC on an area of 16 hectares.
In 2008, the Ministry of Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries changed its name and established the Freshwater Aquaculture Research and Development Center to this day. The center has important roles and activities in research, fish breeding, aquaculture technical training and so on.
The center produces millions of species of fish each year, such as catfish, trout, catfish, tilapia, frogs, lobsters and other species of catfish, for distribution to farmers across the country and no longer enter public waters, community fish ponds to increase both Types and quantities of local fish species.
As of 2020, the Ministry has established 1,190 agricultural communities, of which 165 communities have signed production contracts with the private sector.
Exports of agricultural products have also made significant progress, both officially and informally, amounting to more than 4 million tons. In 2019, animal production reached 45.85 million head, an increase of 3.3% over the previous year, making Cambodia capable of producing meat for domestic consumption of about 239 thousand tons, or 82% of Total meat demand is about 290 thousand tons per year.
Commercial animal husbandry increased by 37% year-on-year due to a change in the behavior of breeders from natural to commercial farming, which resulted in higher revenue growth.
Regarding fisheries resource management and aquaculture development in 2019, the Ministry has so far established 516 fishing communities, including 475 freshwater fishing communities and 41 marine fishing communities.
There are a total of 677 fisheries conservation areas nationwide, 895 community fish ponds, 309 fish hatcheries nationwide, of which 269 are owned by farmers nationwide.
