Understand the law: Listen or record private speech
(Phnom Penh): In daily life, individuals often communicate with each other through face-to-face conversations, cell phone correspondence, social media conversations or other means. Also, some conversations may involve personal issues, family issues, or business secrets that the parties may not or do not want third parties to hear about.
An individual's private life will be violated if those conversations are eavesdropped or copied without prior permission, except as permitted by law. So, to better understand what kind of activities are considered private listening or recording? And to what extent should the person who committed this crime be punished according to the law?
According to the above provisions, no one can hear or record those private or confidential words without the permission of the relevant person or the law. This theft can be done by stealing the camera during a direct conversation or stealing a recording during a telephone conversation.
However, the above article also states that although the parties concerned did not specify the permission to listen or record private speech, but if the parties know that a third party is listening or recording without objection. Or prohibit any action, this action is considered an agreement to listen or record the conversation.
Example: A person has been interviewed by a journalist and he or she has already seen the tape recorder on the interview table, so if the person does not object, the journalist's recording activity will not be considered a crime. No.
In general, anyone who hears and records private or confidential speech without permission shall be punished in accordance with the above-mentioned legal rules. At the same time, in order to avoid violating their privacy rights, every citizen should be careful before discussing any secrets and must immediately express their opposition if they do not want to be eavesdropped or recorded. Any private.
