On July 11, 2020, the Royal Academy of Cambodia also organized a forum to discuss and review the results of Cambodia-US relations over the past 70 years. Cambodia-US relations, which were formally re-established in 1950, were severed in 1963, re-established in 1969, and re-severed in 1975. It was not until 1991 that the relationship was reconnected to the present day.
The 70 years of Cambodian-US relations have been seen as more bitter than soft and erratic. In the last 30 years or so, although Cambodia and the United States have been rated better than 40 years ago, there have been occasional clashes and light-hearted remarks.
At this morning's scientific forum on US-Cambodia relations, keynote speakers, including diplomats, discussed a wide range of communication activities and some of the reasons why Cambodia and the United States have such poor relations.
Dr. Kin Phea, President of the Institute of International Relations of the Royal Academy of Cambodia, sees that the poor relations between Cambodia and the United States are caused by five main problems, including: different policies, China's shadow, internal politics, different views. On issues of democracy, freedom of expression and human rights, and the attitude of diplomats.
Although the relationship between Cambodia and the United States has not improved much over the past 70 years, the United States has noted an important role for development in Cambodia over the last three decades. This too. That role is played by trade, with the two countries' trade volume reaching more than $ 3 billion in recent years and about $ 3 billion in development aid over the next three decades.
Hing Thoraksy, Permanent Secretary of the Office of the Council of Ministers, noted that the United States is an important partner of Cambodia in the development of the country, both in terms of development assistance and as a trading partner, with the United States being a major export market. One of Cambodia.
He added that for the past 70 years, Cambodia and the United States have cooperated in areas such as trade, agriculture, environment, counter-terrorism, military cooperation, education and health.
In this morning's scientific forum, both senior Cambodian officials as well as the US Ambassador expressed their desire to build a better relationship between Cambodia and the United States in the years to come.
Mr. Hing Thoraksy emphasized that the Royal Government of Cambodia warmly welcomes the good and close cooperation between Cambodia and the United States in equal partnership as partners in the regional and international arena towards the steady growth of Building mutual trust between the people and the people as well as the governments of the two countries.
Expressing a desire to build a good relationship with each other is a good thing, but achieving this goal is difficult. What needs to be done and what issues need to be addressed to achieve the goal of building a good relationship?
Principles of democracy, respect for human rights are one of the reasons why Cambodia and the United States are seen as murky. At this point, Dr. Sok Touch, President of the Royal Academy, wants the United States to understand Cambodia without forcing Cambodia to follow suit, because the path to democracy is still young for Cambodia.
In addition to facilitating the democratic process, increasing US investment in Cambodia is also an important channel for improving US-Cambodian relations. Mr. Sok Touch emphasized that no diplomatic work is not profitable. He asserted that foreign policy was not in vain.
"Opening the heart of American investment in Cambodia, as the United States does to other countries, will also lead to greater US influence in Cambodia," Kin Phea said.
