(Phnom Penh): Dr. Kin Phea, President of the Institute of International Relations of the Royal Academy of Cambodia, said that the United States should not be upset when it sees Cambodia close to China and turn to support the development of Cambodia to influence relations with Cambodia.
Mr. Kin Phea said this at a scientific forum on "Cambodia-US relations" for 70 years on Saturday morning, July 11, 2020. Dr. Kin Phea said that the Cambodian-US relationship seems to be tied to the Chinese relationship. He said that on July 9, 1958, when Cambodia began to recognize the People's Republic of China, it was seen as a provocation between Cambodia and the United States.
The President of the Institute of International Relations added that later, especially in the current phase of Cambodia's relations with China, it was seen that as Cambodia became closer to China, Cambodia seemed to have some misunderstandings with the United States.
"Cambodia's foreign policy position is to never consider any country an enemy, but every foreigner can not push Cambodia against the wall because Cambodia, in its constitution, adheres to a policy of neutrality and non-partisanship," he said. Alliance.
Mr. Kin Phea stressed that Cambodia is essential for its socio-economic development through cooperation with all countries in the world. Therefore, foreigners should not be upset just because Cambodia has good relations with any country.
Dr. Kin Phea told foreigners that if they understand that the need for communication comes from investment and development, they should look to Cambodia for help, because Cambodia is in need of help from the international community in all areas.
(Phnom Penh): Mr. Kin Phea, President of the Institute of International Relations of the Royal Academy of Cambodia, sees that there are five reasons why the relationship between Cambodia and the United States is murky and not very good in the past 70 years.
Cambodia-US relations, which were formally re-established in 1950, were severed in 1963, re-established in 1969, and re-severed in 1975. It was not until 1991 that the relationship was reconnected to the present day.
The 70 years of Cambodian-US relations have been seen as more bitter than bland and erratic. In the last 30 years or so, although Cambodia and the United States have been rated better than 40 years ago, there have been times when there have been clashes and tensions.
Forum to discuss and review the results of Cambodia-US relations over the past 70 years, Dr. Kin Phea said that the poor relations between Cambodia and the United States due to different policies between the two countries, shadow China's internal politics, differing views on democracy, freedom of expression and human rights, and the attitude of diplomats.
Mr. Kin Phea explained all five issues as follows:
1: "Different foreign policy": Kin Phea explained that during the Cold War, the United States put in place policies to curb the spread of communism in the region. In particular, Cambodia, as stated in the Constitution, maintains a policy of permanent neutrality and non-alignment. Cambodian policy is to live in peace, live in harmony by all possible means.
2: "Shadow of China": Mr. Kin Phea explained that Cambodia-US relations seem to be linked between Cambodia and China. In 1958, when Samdech Norodom Sihanouk recognized the regime of the People's Republic of China, the diplomatic relations between Cambodia and the United States deteriorated to the point of recalling the US ambassador to Washington.
Subsequently, the relationship between Cambodia and China has developed into a comprehensive strategic partner. The two sides signed an agreement to build a community with a common destiny, especially as Cambodia is seen as China's closest ally in Asia.
3. "Internal politics": Mr. Kin Phea explained that under the UN Constitution, the principle of non-interference in the internal affairs of each country is a fundamental principle in ensuring the stability and stability of inter-state relations. But overall, Cambodian policymakers, or Cambodian politicians, can sometimes see the United States in one image as an internal political affair.
4: "Different views on democracy, the rule of law, human rights": Mr. Kin Phea explained that countries in Asia in general and Cambodia in particular, Cambodia has its own historical context, different culture, and especially the mindset of the Cambodian people. Thus, having different views on the same issue as a source leads to misunderstandings.
5. "Attitude of diplomats": Mr. Kin Phea explained that the attitude of diplomats also plays an important role in the need for highly professional diplomats.
Mr. Kin Phea also expressed his views and views that in order to save or improve the relationship between Cambodia and the United States, Cambodia and the United States must adhere to the principles of the United Nations Charter, non-interference in internal affairs. In each other, by respecting each other, respecting each other's interests, respecting each other's sovereignty and territorial integrity.
Mr. Kin Phea saw that opening the heart of US investment in Cambodia, as the United States does to other countries, will also have a strong influence on the United States.
"Indeed, the development of Cambodia requires the indispensable contribution of all friendly countries," he said. "I believe that understanding and feeling good about each other will make our relationship strong, prosperous and inclusive."
