Letter to readers: From Enemy to Servant
(Phnom Penh): Congratulations to Mr. Suon Serey Ratha on his appointment by the King to be the Secretary of State of the Ministry of Rural Development at the request of the Prime Minister of Cambodia, Samdech Techo Hun Sen. Through this role, it is an opportunity for Mr. Suon Serey Ratha to serve the Royal Government as well as the nation.
Suon Serey Ratha, former president of the Cambodian People's Power Movement, was sentenced to seven years in prison and fined 25 million riel for conspiracy to commit treason, along with three other members of the movement. Because of the power of this verdict, he was deported for many years.
Until May 2015, he was pardoned by the King and returned to the country to engage in political activities through the Khmer Rouge.
In 2017, he was sentenced to prison again after issuing a message criticizing Samdech Techo Hun Sen and the Cambodian army in the border dispute with Laos in Stung Treng province. He was sentenced to five years in prison and fined 10 million riel for insulting the Cambodian military.
After the 2018 national election, Mr. Suon Serey Ratha was pardoned by the King at the request of Samdech Techo Hun Sen.
As far as I remember, Suon Serey Ratha was a staunch opposition politician with the Royal Government of Cambodia led by Samdech Techo Hun Sen. Samdech Techo Hun Sen and his Royal Government have become a scapegoat in Suon Serey Ratha's attack with a lot of rhetoric and harsh words.
But in the end, politicians who dared to speak out against the Prime Minister of Cambodia and the Royal Government of Cambodia also became servants of the Royal Government.
Suon Serey Ratha is not the first opposition politician to turn from a critic of the Prime Minister as well as the Royal Government of Cambodia into a servant of the Royal Government of Cambodia. Samdech Techo Hun Sen.
After the genocidal regime of Pol Pot was overthrown on January 7, 1979, Samdech Techo Hun Sen had many fighting partners, both armed and political. But in the end, most of the armed and political fighters were given a chance and turned from enemies to serve and coexist with the government led by Samdech Techo Hun Sen.
Conflicts in Cambodian political society, historically, did not occur only during the reign of Samdech Techo Hun Sen, but for hundreds of thousands of years. Conflict is less likely to heal because in that conflict less dharma and compassion are used. Only death for the loser and the winner can survive, so Cambodian society is divided.
But since 1979, Samdech Techo Hun Sen has been considered a new generation of Cambodian leaders who use high virtues of life, compassion and high tradition for their enemies. Because of the virtues of life, because of the compassion in this life, and this is why this leader always gives grace to all enemies to come and live with him, no matter how much they used to insult him.
And perhaps the leader's adherence to the non-violent dharma of compassion was the only way to put an end to the disintegration of the Khmer people and to find historical unity in the Cambodian nation in the new era since the fall of Angkor during 1296. From there.
By: Suburbs
