In the early stages, the Spanish flu invaded Europe, the United States, and parts of Asia before spreading rapidly around the world. At that time, there was no drug or vaccine to treat this type of flu. People were ordered to wear masks, and schools, cinemas and businesses were closed while corpses were congested before the disease disappeared on its own.
* The emergence of the Spanish flu, a new type of flu
The first epidemic of the Spanish flu began in the early 1918s, but did not appear to be severe. Most infected people only show mild flu symptoms, such as fever and fatigue, and recover in the next few days while the death toll is low.
According to experts, the cause of the Spanish flu massacre was due to the fact that the health sector at that time was still very weak, with no respiratory aids or means to develop a vaccine. In addition, measures were taken to prevent the spread of the disease, and the spread of the disease was less widespread, including during World War I.
To this day, scientists still do not know the exact origin of the Spanish flu, with only theories and doubts pointing to France, China, the United Kingdom or the United States.
The Spanish flu epidemic ended in the mid-1920s, when the epidemic ceased to exist on its own, and most infected people developed antibodies to protect themselves from the disease.
(Kratie): Mr. Try Meng, Secretary of State of the Ministry of Rural Development, and the participation of Mr. Khan Chamnan, Deputy Provincial Governor and relevant departments and units on July 10, 2020, held a meeting to review the results of the first half of the year. 020 of Kratie Provincial Water Supply and Sanitation Technical Working Group. The meeting was conducted by the Department of Rural Development and supported by Plan International and EMW at the Kratie Provincial Hall.
Water supply in urban and urban areas of the province has improved significantly, especially in recent years, water supply to rural people through the construction of water supply infrastructure, including: Pump wells, community ponds, rainwater filters, community communities and community water distribution systems that improve people's access to drinking water.
