The goal of Cambodia's national development, which focuses on the development of the education sector, has given Cambodian youth a wide range of educational opportunities. That study includes both domestic and international studies. Many Cambodian students have studied and are studying abroad, whether in ASEAN, Asia, Europe or the United States.
China, for example, is one of many countries that regularly provide scholarships to Cambodian students to study for free. Currently, there are more than 2,000 Cambodian students studying and graduating each year, more than 300 in China. This is according to the report from the Cambodian Students Association in the People's Republic of China.
As a Cambodian student studying in China in Guangxi Zhuang Autonomous Region, Ms. Tang Xuhuang said that because China and Cambodia have had a long relationship, it made her want to learn more about China until she decided to take a food exam. Scholarship to study in China.
However, Ms. Tang Suhuang still wants to return to Cambodia after graduation. She came back because she wanted to come and help develop Cambodia.
In addition to continuing their studies to absorb new knowledge from the education sector in China, Cambodian students also introduced Cambodian culture and history to the Chinese people. All of these factors have made it possible for Chinese people of all walks of life to access information about Cambodia through Cambodian students studying there.
For example, Mr. Nhel Soviet, President of the Cambodian Students Association in Yunnan Province, China, who represents 215 Cambodian students there, said that all Cambodian students often participate in showcasing Cambodian culture through programs organized by schools and short programs. Many more. With the support of the Cambodian Embassy, the Consulate General of Cambodia in Yuna Province, as well as Mr. HUN Manet and Mr. HUN Many.
Cambodian students studying in China, in addition to promoting their national culture during the Khmer New Year, also participated in promoting their culture in various short programs. This proves that no matter where Cambodians are, they do not forget their cultural traditions.
On the other hand, in addition to promoting their national culture, Cambodian students also learn about the traditional culture of the Chinese people in the region. For example, Nhel Soviet, a doctoral candidate studying in Yunnan Province, said that Yunnan Province in southern China is an agricultural province that can supply the whole of China. The most famous in Yuna is tea.
According to Ms. Vuth Sopheakna, President of the Confucius Institute in Cambodia, the Institute was established on eight missions, including:
The Confucius Institute offers short courses in general knowledge of Chinese, undergraduate Chinese language and HSK and HSKK Chinese language proficiency test preparation courses, and has 22 branches in Cambodia. For the past 10 years, the institute has sent 500 students to study in China at 213 universities, all of which are well-known universities in China. It also enrolls about 140,000 students each year.
In addition to providing training and scholarships to students once a year, the Confucius Institute also organizes study tours for civil servants and a summer / winter camp program for students.
