The following is the Royal Decree: Cambodia and South Korea begin negotiations on a free trade agreement
(Phnom Penh): After months of feasibility study, Cambodia and South Korea have finally started negotiations on a free trade agreement with each other on Thursday, July 9, 2020.
The opening of talks on a free trade agreement between Cambodia and South Korea was made via video by the Cambodian side, led by Minister of Commerce Pan Sosak, and the South Korean side, led by YOO Myung-hee. Myung-Hee) South Korean Minister of Commerce.
Prior to today's official opening of the talks, the two countries' working groups met several times via video message and went through several stages, including: 1) A joint study on the feasibility of Establishment of a bilateral free trade agreement; 2) the establishment of a common principle of negotiation; and 3) the request for a permit from the governments of the two countries.
Studies show that the Cambodia-South Korea Free Trade Agreement will be a catalyst to strengthen economic and political ties, increase trade and investment, increase gross domestic product (GDP), benefit consumers as well as strengthen Cooperation between institutions of the two countries.
The trade volume between Cambodia and South Korea in 2019 was about 1,032 million US dollars, an increase of 254% compared to 2009 which was only about 291 million US dollars. The two countries' trade volume has grown by an average of 15% per year over the last 10 years.
At today's meeting, the two trade ministers expressed optimism and high commitment to achieving this bilateral free trade agreement, which shows full support for the multilateral trading system. Relying on legal principles and especially to help the economy recover quickly after the Kovid-19 crisis.
The two ministers also discussed and exchanged views on the status and development of the World Trade Organization (WTO) and expressed common views on continuing to support the WTO and the multilateral trading system that has played a role. Important in helping member countries, both developing and underdeveloped, including Cambodia.
At the same time, the negotiations to establish a free trade agreement that the Ministry of Commerce has been implementing in accordance with the "Political Program of the Royal Government of the Sixth Legislature of the National Assembly" and the "Rectangular Strategy Phase 4" under the wise leadership. Of Samdech Techo Hun Sen, Prime Minister of Cambodia, aims to further diversify Cambodia's export markets and products.
To promote trade and economic diversification, Cambodia has put in place new strategies to expand trade with friendly countries. Free trade is a strategy that the Royal Government of Cambodia has been working with friendly countries today.
The Ministry of Commerce, a government agency, has set up eight working groups to formulate policies and strategies for negotiating free trade agreements with China, the United Kingdom, the Republic of Korea, the Eurasian Economic Union (EAEU), the United States, Japan, Mongolia and India.
Negotiations for a free trade agreement with China have now been concluded and documents have been submitted to the two countries' leaders for review and approval. A free trade agreement between Cambodia and China is expected to be signed by the end of 2020.
