Australia extends visas to Hong Kong students and citizens for up to five years and applies for permanent residency
Sydney: Australian Prime Minister Scott Morrison on Thursday announced the extension of visas for Hong Kong nationals in Australia for up to five years, as well as suspending extradition agreements with Hong Kong. In response to Beijing passing a new security law for the Asian Business Center. This is according to the release by CNA on Thursday afternoon, July 9, 2020.
Morrison has announced that Hong Kong students graduating from Australia will have the opportunity to stay for another five years. They will then be able to apply for permanent residency in Australia. Hong Kong citizens holding temporary work visas will also be eligible to stay for the same five years, after which they will be able to apply for permanent residency.
The prime minister also announced the suspension of an extradition agreement with Hong Kong on the grounds that the new national security law for Hong Kong, passed by the Chinese parliament last week, was a fundamental change in the situation. Currently, because the Hong Kong suspects can be sent to court in the courts run by the mainland Chinese Communist Party.
Ministry of Social Affairs holds a meeting to review the implementation of the action plan to prevent and respond to violence against children
(Phnom Penh): The Ministry of Social Affairs, Veterans and Youth Rehabilitation on July 9, 2020 held a meeting to review the implementation of the action plan to prevent and respond to violence against children 2017-2021.
The meeting has two main agendas: first: review and approval of the annual summary report for 2019 and second: review of key indicators and report collection forms for monitoring the implementation of action plans to prevent and Responding to violence against children in 2017-2021.
In addition, members of the Technical Working Group reviewed and commented on key key indicators of the Action Plan for the Prevention and Response to Violence against Children 2017-2021, which are indicators to monitor the action plan.
The working group identified 20 indicators to facilitate the monitoring and reporting of progress and implementation challenges, as well as to discuss the draft report form to gather information on the progress of these 20 indicators so that we can produce A regular report (quarterly and annually) that measures the progress of implementation and shares experiences with each other.
Reporting possibilities and reporting guidelines for these indicators will be met through a steering committee meeting to review and finalize.
Afterwards, the members of the technical working group will discuss the procedures or procedures for collecting reports on the implementation of the plan from the ministries, member institutions, commissions and partner organizations for 2020 and 2021.
Finally, the Secretariat will update the mid-term evaluation plan of the Action Plan to Prevent and Respond to Violence against Children 2017-2021. Over the past six months, more than 2,000 chainsaws have been seized in forest crime across Cambodia
