(Battambang): The President of the Cambodian Veterans Association of Battambang Province has been replaced by Mr. Meas Sovann, while Mr. Ouk Khnouch, the former President of the Association has been transferred to another position.
The change of president of the association was held last week under the chairmanship of Senior Minister Kun Kim, Secretary General of the Veterans Association and Mr. Nguon Rattanak, Battambang Provincial Governor and Honorary President of the Association.
On that occasion, Mr. Ouk Khnouch, who was transferred from the President of the Cambodian Veterans Association in Battambang Province, presented some of the achievements that the Association achieved during his tenure. At the same time, Mr. Meas Sovann, who was newly appointed, also committed to lead the association as well as serve the veterans to the best of their ability and ability to provide warmth to the veterans.
On that occasion, Senior Minister Kun Kim conveyed the greetings of Samdech Techo Hun Sen, Prime Minister of Cambodia and President of the Veterans Association and Samdech Kittiprittbandit Bun Rany Hun Sen, President of the Cambodian Red Cross to the leaders and veterans in All Battambang provinces.
At the same time, the Secretary General of the Cambodian Veterans Association praised Mr. Ouk Khnouch, who, during his tenure as the President of the Association, worked hard with the Association's leaders in other provinces to perform their duties well and solve challenges for the former. The fighters were on time and put in place other measures to improve the lives of the veterans.
On that occasion, Senior Minister Kun Kim introduced and reminded Mr. Meas Sovann, the new president of the association and the leaders of the provincial association to work together to perform their duties better to serve the veterans. In particular, address the challenges of veterans in a timely manner.
The Senior Minister also asked the former President of the Association, Mr. Ouk Khnouch, to continue to help the Cambodian Veterans Association of Battambang Province because he already has experience and knows the deep work of more than 10,000 veterans in the province.
Senior Minister Kun Kim also reminded the veterans to take care of hygiene, implement measures to prevent Kovit-19 as a good example at the grassroots level, and strive to make a living, not to rely on government subsidies in the vulnerable areas by Kovit. 19 This.
