National Bank: Cambodia maintains good macroeconomic and financial stability despite the Kovid 19 crisis!
(Phnom Penh): The National Bank of Cambodia (NBC) claims that Cambodia has maintained good macroeconomic and financial stability despite the current crisis of 19 Kovid. This is according to the summary report for the first half of the year and set the direction for the second half of 2020
In line with the policy of the Royal Government, the National Bank of Cambodia has set out the policy of the Royal Government by setting out the policy of monetary policy, innovation and measures in the banking sector to increase cash in the banking sector to meet the need for financing to support economic activities. And encourage credit restructuring to maintain business sustainability and for the hardships of affected people.
"Despite these impacts, Cambodia's macroeconomic and financial stability continues to be good," the report said. Inflation included a low of 2.5 percent due to lower prices for fuel-related goods and services, which eased the pressure on rising food prices. "The riel exchange rate against the US dollar has been stable, albeit slightly higher."
Meanwhile, international reserves have continued to increase and can guarantee the import of goods and services for the next 10 months, higher than the minimum level for developing countries should be 3 months.
"Meanwhile, the banking sector continued to grow confidently and actively contributed to economic activity, with loans continuing to grow by 19.6 percent and deposits by 12.3 percent," the report said. The banking and financial institutions are healthy and have gradually expanded their network of operations and financial services and improved the financial environment in a sustainable manner.
World Bank: Cambodian Researcher Rewarded for Innovative Approaches to Help Prevent and Respond to Gender-Based Violence
"Gender equality is a high priority for the World Bank in Cambodia, and it is reflected as a strong theme within the framework of our Country Partnership," said Ingonado Pracha, World Bank Country Manager for Cambodia. We. "KHANA's project is an important effort to find ways to keep those female employees safe, especially those at high risk."
During the 19 global epidemics of Kovid, evidence emerged that violence against women and girls, especially domestic violence, was on the rise around the world as a result of house arrests. Infection and due to lack of access to services at this time.
The World Health Organization estimates that one in three women has experienced sexual violence from a partner or non-partner in their lifetime. Many researchers have found that gender stereotypes are often at the root of gender-based violence, and that changing community attitudes is important to help prevent violence against women and girls.
"The impact of gender-based violence is not limited to the immediate aftermath of a victim attack, but it stays with them for a long time," said Karen Green, the World Bank Group's Global Gender Director. "These include health problems, frequent absences, loss of productivity in the workplace, loss of income for the family and long-term negative effects on the next generation."
The World Organization for Animal Welfare supports Siem Reap to become the first province to ban dog meat
FOUR PAWS has been working closely with the Royal Government of Cambodia since 2018, advocating for a ban on the dog meat trade due to public health risks, mostly caused by rabies and the spread of infectious diseases. Come animals.
Dr. Catherine Polack, Veterinarian and Head of Homeless Care in Southeast Asia, confirms that the Siem Reap government's decision to ban piracy, trade and the killing of dogs is a major event for animal welfare. In the country and reflect the current feelings of the Cambodian people.
She hopes that Siem Reap will set an example for other provinces to follow in order to save the lives of millions of dogs, while during the investigation it was found that some restaurants in Siem Reap were selling dog meat. Well.
According to research by FOUR PAWS, live dogs cost from $ 2 to $ 3 per kilogram, while raw meat sells for up to $ 4 per kilogram. Most of the eaters are men who tend to eat dog meat as a snack with friends accompanied by alcohol. Women who eat dog meat, on the other hand, tend to eat dog meat at home and because it is believed to be a cure.
