Efforts to preserve and promote to the international community the cultural treasures and ancient temples built by Khmer ancestors are one of the main goals of the Royal Government of Cambodia led by Samdech Techo Hun Sen. In this spirit, many of Cambodia's cultural treasures and ancient temple sites have been sought by the Royal Government of Cambodia to be submitted to the World Heritage Organization for inclusion in the World Heritage List.
The placement of these cultural treasures and ancient temples not only received international protection, but also helped to promote Khmer cultural treasures and ancient temples to the international stage.
Sambor Prei Kuk is another Cambodian temple after Angkor Wat and Preah Vihear were added to the World Heritage Committee's list of World Heritage Sites at the 41st meeting in Krakow, Poland.
Samdech Techo Hun Sen emphasized on his Facebook page this morning, July 8, 2020 that after being registered as a World Heritage Site, Sambor Prei Kuk Temple has become an attractive cultural and historical tourist site.
Samdech Techo Hun Sen has emphasized that the achievement of the inclusion of Sambor Prei Kuk in the World Heritage List is the result of the Royal Government of Cambodia's efforts. In particular, Ms. Phoeung Sakna, Minister of Culture and Fine Arts, a staff member of the Royal Government of Cambodia.
Samdech Techo, the head of the Royal Government, has emphasized that the inclusion of the Sambor Prei Kuk temple in the World Heritage List is a source of pride for the Cambodian nation. To celebrate the inclusion of the Sambor Prei Kuk and Preah Vihear temples in the World Heritage List, Samdech Techo Hun Sen has decided to mark July 10 as the National Day of Remembrance for the inclusion of these two temples in the World Heritage List. Also.
Please note that Sambor Prei Kuk Temple is located in Sambo Village, Sambo Commune, Prasat Sambo District, Kampong Thom Province, 25 km from Kampong Thom Provincial Town on National Road 64, turn right onto Kon Tnaot Road. The temple is the ancient capital of Isanpura, which at that time was the center of a religion dedicated to Shiva. This group of temples is built of solid brick, laterite and sandstone.
Sambor Prei Kuk was built by King Isanvarman I in the 7th century AD. According to the Sanskrit and Khmer inscriptions, the capital was named "Isanpur" in his own name. Importantly, during his reign, rule was based on politics and religion, so during his reign many temples were built.
Among these temples, too, in terms of style, the lotus flower or lotus flower as an ornament is very important in Khmer beliefs to this day, "representing purity and holiness."
During the war in the 1970s, the Sambor Prei Kuk temple complex was once again isolated and threatened by nature.
