In it, they found that in our world, there are 10 major bloody wars that have killed the most people in the history of mankind, especially it left a tragedy for the whole world.
Want to know what happened in those wars, when and when they ended, and why?
The origins of this enormous period began in early 1939, after Germany invaded Austria and Czechoslovakia, the European powers that won World War I, France and Britain, began to realize Hitler's expansionist ambitions.
On August 22, the Soviet Union and Germany signed a non-aggression pact, with a secret clause stating that during the war between Germany and Poland, the Soviet Union could seize territory in eastern Poland and three other countries. Around the Baltic Sea are Latvia, Lithuania and Estonia. The fate of Poland was determined not by fate but by the Hitler-Stalin Accords.
Hitler planned for the German army to attack two targets simultaneously in order to occupy the Polish city of Warsaw. But Hitler still needed a reasonable excuse to invade Poland.
On the night of August 31, 1939, German commandos in Polish uniform launched an attack on several positions in German territory near the border with Poland. Hitler claimed that Poland was the attacker on Germany before sending troops to invade Poland in the early hours of September 1.
On September 3, France and Britain set a deadline for Hitler to withdraw his troops from Poland. Later that night, the deadline expired. Hitler did not withdraw his troops. France and Britain declared war on Germany, and World War II broke out.
Second: Mongol War of Expansion (1206-1368) This war took place in the early 13th century, a war that extended the territory of the Mongol Empire, covering much of Asia and Eastern Europe to the middle. 1300 Attacks and invasions of Mongol territory were the deadliest conflicts, with up to 60 million killed.
When Genghis Khan was born and raised, Mongolia was not yet a united nation. The Mongols lived in isolated tribes, sometimes at war with one another. From the 7th century, the Mongols were under Chinese rule in the Tang Dynasty and later under the Liao Dynasty.
In 1215, Genghis Khan's army launched an offensive against Beijing. After capturing the city, the Mongol army burned down houses and massacred almost all the people. North China also fell under the control of the Mongol Empire.
The cause of the outbreak of the First World War in 1914 is rooted in the many problems that arose decades ago, such as the war between France and Russia in 1870-1871, the emergence of the ambitious German Empire. Becoming a colonial power, competing with other European powers.
However, the events that ignited the war came from the assassination of Prince Ferdinand, heir to the Austro-Hungarian Empire, in Sarajevo on June 28, 1914. Sarajevo is a Bosnian city that Austria-Hungary annexed from the Ottoman Empire to serve as a province under Serbia.
Looking back 76 years ago, on September 2, 1945, Japan formally surrendered to World War II, after the Japanese emperor Hirohito declared war on August 15, 1945. Japan's surrender was the culmination of World War II.
In 1917, a year after the Second Revolution, the Socialist Bolsheviks captured the political heart of Russia. They rejected the Constituent Assembly elected by terrorists and banned opposition politics. It is clear that they want a dictatorship.
8th Vietnam War (1955-1975) The Civil War between South Vietnam and North Vietnam was known as the Second Indochina War. North Vietnam is fully supported by China and some communist countries, and South Vietnam is backed by the United States along with other anti-communist countries. More than 1 million civilians have been killed in that society.
On April 21, 1975, when North Vietnamese troops invaded the outskirts of Saigon, President Nguyen Van Thieu announced his resignation with tears in his eyes before fleeing to Taiwan, condemning the United States. That betrayed South Vietnam, tricked him into signing the Paris Agreement, promising to continue providing military assistance but not complying.
On April 29, the United States evacuated all its embassy officials and staff from South Vietnam, and a day later on April 30, Saigon fell to the North Vietnamese army and the Republic of Vietnam. The south also collapsed completely.
In 1979, the Soviet Union decided to try its luck in Afghanistan, the target of Russian foreign policy. Many historians believe that, in the end, the Soviet Union in Afghanistan was the key to overthrowing the two powers of the Cold War.
An estimated 850,000 to 1.5 million Afghan civilians have died in the war and five to ten million have fled the country as refugees. This number represents one-third of the population in 1978, which hit Pakistan and other neighboring countries. Twenty-five thousand Afghans were killed by landmines during the war, and millions of mines remained behind after the Soviet withdrawal.
10th - American Civil War (1861-1865) The American Civil War was joined by North and South America to break the existing slave states within America. The Civil War, or separatism, unified the United States and abolished slavery throughout the United States, killing more than a million people.
The American Civil War broke out between the 23 northern states, with a population of more than 20 million, an army of about 2 million, and advanced industrial and modern infrastructure against 11 southern states. The state has a population of just over 9 million and an army of just over 1 million, but is rich in warlords.
By 1864, the armies of the northern states had the upper hand over the southern states. On April 9, 1865, the army of the Southern Confederates declared their surrender, but five days later Abraham Lincoln was shot dead by a Southern State supporter while sitting in a theater in Washington, DC
The American Civil War lasted more than four years, and the northern and southern states were united as a single federal state. In total, more than 1 million Americans in both the southern and northern states lost their lives, about 3% of the total population in the United States at the time.
