Expert accountant explains the issue of revenue and expenditure distribution among related persons and solutions within the tax law
These enterprises do business in many countries and also allocate resources such as labor, raw materials, intellectual property and other resources to each country. Some enterprises have subsidiaries in Cambodia. The subsidiaries buy raw materials from the parent company and sell the product back to the parent company overseas.
Distributing income and expenses among these stakeholders is sometimes a challenge for those enterprises, especially tax compliance. Today, I would like to introduce Mr. San Chankumpheak, an accountant who specializes in auditing, accounting and taxation, who came in to explain the following easy-to-understand technical questions and answers:
1: What is the distribution of income and expenses among the people involved?
When it comes to the distribution of income and expenses among stakeholders, it refers to two keywords. The first is the transfer fee and the second is the person involved. Transfer fee refers to the cost of goods, services or property supplied among related parties. The person involved has two characteristics that we can notice.
According to the Prakas of the Ministry of Economy and Finance on Rules and Procedures for the Distribution of Income and Expenditure among Related Persons No. 986 MEF.PrK dated 20 October 2017, it is clearly stated that the relevant persons refer to:
A: Any member of the taxpayer's family. The term "management" means ownership of 20% or more of the value of the share in the direct capital of the natural person and the direct or indirect ownership of the taxpayer spouse.
This has led to various laws and regulations to ensure that the distribution of income and expenditure among the parties is in accordance with the terms and conditions of the policy, to ensure that there is no reduction in prices to avoid paying taxes to the state. Enterprises must also comply with other laws and regulations to avoid severe tax penalties.
From practical experience, we observe that enterprises that are subsidiaries of foreign enterprises in Cambodia find it difficult to explain to the General Department of Taxation that transactions with their respective persons are based on the principle of hands-on approach.
As for the principle of hand standing, I would like to give an example.
This principle deals with two key keywords: unorganized operations and organized operations.
3: How many ways do they have a way to divide revenue and expenditure under the hand-held principle?
Pursuant to Article 18 of the Tax Law, in order to divide the income and expenditure among the relevant persons under the guiding principle, the taxpayer may use one or more of the following methods:
Please note that the General Department of Taxation requires taxpayers to prepare and provide evidence and supporting documents for the chosen method. If necessary, the tax administration has the right to instruct and direct the taxpayer to use any method that suits the actual situation of the taxpayer.
I can explain as follows:
5. What documents must the taxpayer prepare to occupy and keep in accordance with the Prakas on the rules and procedures for the distribution of income and expenses among the relevant persons?
Taxpayers who operate with related persons have the obligation to properly prepare the following information: a: General information of the enterprise and related persons, such as documents confirming the structure and relations among the affiliated enterprises, documents and reports on development strategies And business and regulations and procedures related to the management and preparation of financial statements.
B: Information on the operation of the enterprise, such as diagrams and documents describing the operation, including information about the parties and the terms and conditions of supply, delivery, payment, etc. It is also included in the cost document as a unit information, inventory, sales contract, market information that affects the production cost of the product and so on.
C: Information on how to transfer fees, such as price policy, supporting documents for choosing the method of transfer, documents as evidence of the basis and position of negotiations between taxpayers and affiliated enterprises related to business operations and other documents. Regarding the transfer fee.
Due to the complex nature of document management, which requires organizers to have multidisciplinary knowledge, such as accounting, taxation, economics, international trade law, etc., taxpayers should seek out a professional advisor accredited by the General Department of Taxation, National Accounting Council. And the Cambodian Institute of Professional Accountants and Auditors.
Please be informed that Mr. San Chankompheak is a professional accountant and auditor recognized by the General Department of Taxation, the National Accounting Council and the Institute of Professional Accountants and Auditors of Cambodia.
