Because Mandela, one of many blacks, was made to oppress and despise the law, the future president of South Africa was in great pain and suffering. Intended to save its people from the clutches of ruthless whites.
* About entering politics:
The law on racial discrimination requires blacks to be the true owners of the country, becoming second citizens, resulting in segregation of rights and castes. The law required blacks to have no political rights to pursue a career as required by the government, or to work with whites for less than whites.
In addition, the law limits some of the worst things that can happen: black children cannot go to school with white children, black people do not have the right to sit in public places like whites, Blacks only have access to stores for blacks.
* About 28 years of detention:
Mandela initially chose to use non-violent demonstrations like Mahatma Gandhi, but later could not stand the sight of white soldiers using violence against protesters, he decided to form an armed force called the South African National Assembly to Demands freedom and freedom to govern their country. Mandela was jailed on charges of plotting to overthrow the government and wreaking havoc on the nation.
* About being released from prison to become president:
The white government mistakenly thought that the arrest and imprisonment of Mandela would frighten blacks, but on the contrary, Africans of all nationalities began to react more and more to the point of bloodshed. . The riots prompted the white government to ease racist laws, but still failed to quell black anger.
Recently released from prison, Mandela's reputation rose to prominence and he was awarded the Nobel Peace Prize in 1993 after three years in prison. He was then elected President of South Africa on May 9, 1994, after four years in prison. When leading the country, the president strives to perform his duties without fear of exhaustion, even in his old age.
