Koh Kong provincial officials found chemicals in nearly 2 kilos of soybean foam products that traders sell in the flag market
(Koh Kong): Officers of the Consumer Protection, Competition and Anti-Fraud Branch (CPC) in Koh Kong Province on July 1, 2020, cooperated with the provincial trade officials to inspect the quality and safety of food products, especially Some high-risk items, such as bubble beans, dried beans, trout, which traders display in Dang Tong market, Khemarak Phoumin, Koh Kong province.
Mr. Chea Ny, Sub-Branch of Consumer Protection, Competition and Anti-Fraud, Koh Kong Province, said that the inspection of food safety, especially high-risk goods such as bubble beans, dried beans that traders import from Phnom Penh to sell in Dang Tong Market: Officials from the provincial branch of the CPP inspected 8 boxes of dried soybean foam and found 1 bottle of banned chemical (Borax) on 1.3 kg of soybean foam products.
He said that after testing for borax, experts called the owner to make a contract and advise him to stop accepting resale, and before buying the goods to sell to check the correct date of manufacture. As for the soybean foam that was tested and contained 1.3 kilograms of boracic acid, the provincial branch officials took it to be stored for destruction.
Mr. Chea Ny also called on the business community to cooperate to provide information, if any location or stall selling groceries after the date of the date to report to the provincial branch officials immediately to prevent Do not affect health.
