(Stung Treng): Mr. Say Sam Al, Minister of Environment, stressed that local authorities have an obligation to protect state land from illegal encroachment and have a responsibility to protect and conserve natural resources in their area.
Mr. Say Sam Al said so at the meeting on the management of state public land in Stung Treng Provincial Protected Area, which was held on July 1, 2020 at Stung Treng Provincial Hall with the participation of provincial authorities. Districts, communes and relevant institutions of Stung Treng province and members of the Third Committee of the Senate and the National Assembly also participated.
He said that through efforts to better protect and conserve natural resources, so far large-scale natural resource crimes no longer exist, but small-scale crimes that all relevant authorities need to continue. Collaborate to prevent and suppress effectively for the sustainability of the environment and natural resources.
In this regard, Mr. Mom Saroeun, Governor of Stung Treng Province, mentioned the challenges in the management of the six protected areas, especially the encroachment of land in the Prey Lang Wildlife Sanctuary. Siem Pang, Siem Pang Vern Sai National Park and the Northeast Biodiversity Conservation Corridor.
According to professional officials, encroachment on state land in Thala Parivat district has been rampant and even until state land has been split into lots for sale, and research shows that local authorities, including village chiefs and commune chiefs, are involved in Sign and recognize the illegally occupied land. This act is against the law, and those involved have been building a case to be sent to court for legal action.
In front of Stung Treng provincial authorities, Minister Say Sam Al said that the commune authorities should distinguish between the clearing of land in the old village where the people live in accordance with the principle of tiger skin and the request to cut the land from the encroachment on state land. Illegally.
At the same time, Mr. Say Sam Al also suggested to the authorities to consult with the community to create new business options such as animal husbandry and other agricultural crops in addition to traditional occupations, confiscation of non-timber forest products, land encroachment, nomadic plantations, and the Ministry of Environment is ready to help. Techniques and budgets.
At the same time, the Minister praised the Yangtze Rice Project implemented by BirdLife International with the people in the Siem Pang Wildlife Sanctuary in the direction of linking conservation and creating a local economy by stopping hunting and logging. And encroachment on the forest land of the people in that area.
"I really appreciate the families who have stopped logging, hunting and encroaching on forest land and turned to organic rice, which is marketed and sold at a high price," he said. This is a testament to the changing attitudes of the people towards the environment and natural resources, and to see more and more life changes. "This is what we need to do and create activities to help the community."
Mr. Say Sam Al noted that through education, dissemination of knowledge and participation from all stakeholders, we observe that most Cambodians have become environmentalists, love natural resources and participate in protecting nature.
The Minister also ordered the relevant authorities to identify loggers and hunters for education, guidance and plans to transform them from natural resource destroyers to participants in the protection and conservation of natural resources with professional officials by creating Occupation and income for him.
