The new National Security Law, which has six chapters and 66 articles, stipulates four types of crimes, including: "Separation, subversion, terrorism and conspiracy with foreigners, as well as domestic forces undermining security." National. Under the new law, masterminds and perpetrators can face up to life in prison or up to 10 years in prison, or more for the four types of crimes.
However, China and the Hong Kong government remain adamant that the law is aimed only at criminals and will not undermine the freedom of the people of Hong Kong. Instead, the law will help strengthen Hong Kong's security and order, as well as its "one country, two systems" policy.
On the other hand, although Washington has taken a number of measures, such as visa restrictions on Chinese officials and the removal of Hong Kong's special status as an Asian financial center in accordance with US law, China still has passed the law. Why did China override Hong Kong's national security law, knowing that the United States and the West would take action?
Pro-Beijing Hong Kong politicians and some Chinese policy advisers have called for the new national security law to be enforced and to maintain order, as well as to prevent the resumption of protests like in 2019. But some experts believe that China's goal may be more than that: China wants Hong Kong like Macau.
In addition, according to former Hong Kong senior official Lau Siu-kai, China's passage of a new national security law for Hong Kong also involves US action. The current conflict and deepening tensions between the United States and China have made Chinese Communist Party leaders increasingly concerned about Western influence in Hong Kong.
Under the July 1, 1997 agreement between Beijing and Britain to return Hong Kong to China, China did not have full control of Hong Kong until a "one country, one system" policy. Two "will expire in 2047, when China has full control of Hong Kong."
(Kampong Speu): Mr. Kao Thach, Director General of the Bank for Rural Development and Agriculture, on July 1, 2020, encouraged people to seize the golden opportunity of raising pigs because Vietnam is in dire need of pigs.
Vietnam is currently facing a shortage of pigs for domestic supply due to the African swine fever epidemic, which has killed millions of Vietnamese pigs.
This statement was made in a program to promote credit to farmers, animal husbandry, aquaculture, fruit and vegetable processing and agricultural processing enterprises under the chairmanship of Mr. Vy Samnang, Governor of Kampong Speu this morning, July 1, 2020.
Mr. Kao Thach stated, "You see, the price of pigs has gone up because of Kovid 19, because our domestic supply is not enough, it may be lacking." "Everyone is raising pigs because there are so many big markets. China needs more pigs, Vietnam needs more pigs."
"Vietnam is facing a shortage of pigs for domestic supply due to the plague, which has killed millions of African pigs, making it difficult to raise them," he said. "I think this is a great opportunity to build a foundation for safe, non-chemical agriculture."
At the same time, Mr. Kao Thach also asked all farmers to pay attention to health, not to use excessive chemicals, while neighboring countries use excessive chemicals that affect the health of the people.
