(Pursat): With financial support from local and foreign philanthropists, Venerable Lay Kan, Chief Bishop of Wat Bakan, invited a total of 400 monks and nuns from all pagodas throughout Bakan district to provide food to increase intimacy between the gods. Priests and monks while Kovid 19 was slightly relieved of the failure to hold traditional ceremonies.
The ceremony was held at Wat Bakan, Trapeang Chong commune, Bakan district, Pursat province recently, with the participation of Mr. Run Sophearin, Director of the Provincial Department of Cult and Religion, and a large number of Buddhists.
Venerable Lay Kan, Chief Bishop of Bakan Pagoda, said that he had invited all 42 pagoda bishops throughout Bakan district, as well as a total of 400 monks and nuns, to come and receive food to unite as a family of monks. One like the family of 490,000 people organized by Mr. Mao Thunin, Governor of Pursat. Doing this to increase intimacy, know how to grieve and help each other when something goes wrong.
According to the Chief Bishop of Wat Bakan, the food provided to the monks received $ 500 from the royal family, $ 600 from Ms. Teng Gek Nai, as well as Buddhists from inside and outside the pagoda.
Venerable Lay Kan said that in 2020, we will hold a gathering to give food to only one monk in Bakan district, but in 2021, if we can afford it, we will do it all over the province with a total of more than 2,000 monks.
On behalf of the leadership of the Provincial Department of Cult and Religion, Mr. Run Sophearin praised Venerable Lay Kan, Chief Bishop of Wat Bakan, who led and managed the presence of remarkable progress, good relations with leaders and philanthropists from all over. As well as raising and training hundreds of ordinary students from generation to generation.
Not only that, he participated in the humanitarian field, provided food, equipment and money to the Red Cross branch, distributed gifts to the poor, the sick, the orphans, the army and helped build the temple of Sala Chan Kod to a remote pagoda.
The director of the department added that Buddhism is the state religion that we all believe in and obey because Buddhism provides psychological education to do good deeds and help each other to live in harmony in society. One by harmony.
