(Phnom Penh): Mr. Sam Rainsy on February 11, 2017 decided to resign as the former President of the National Rescue Party. He claimed that the decision to resign was made to prevent the dissolution of the CNRP under the rule of law on political parties in Cambodia.
Following the announcement of Sam Rainsy's resignation the following month, on March 2, 2017, Kem Sokha was voted in by the party to replace Sam Rainsy.
Despite the change of party president under the pretext of maintaining the CNRP's presence not to be dissolved by the law on political parties in Cambodia, the party was eventually dissolved by the Cambodian Supreme Court, according to a ministry complaint. Interior on November 17, 2017. The Ministry of Interior has accused the political party of seeking to overthrow the government and violating the law on political parties.
Until now, the dissolution of the CNRP has become a hot topic in the debate between former CNRP supporters between those who have a husband with Kem Sokha and those who have a husband with Sam Rainsy.
Mr. Sam Rainsy's husband is considered as a courageous resignation of the party president by sacrificing everything to maintain the party's presence. They also attacked Kem Sokha, saying that Kran Niel with the party's position refused to resign until the party was dissolved.
For those who have a husband who is loyal to Mr. Kem Sokha, they do not understand that. Hing Soksan, former Youth President of the Cambodia National Rescue Party, at The Cambodia Daily forum on the night of June 29, 2020, came out to attack Sam Rainsy and reveal some secrets about the resignation of the President of the Cambodia National Rescue Party. Of Mr. Sam Rainsy as well.
Hing Soksan called the claim that Sam Rainsy resigned as party president a sacrifice to save the party was merely a hoax. He stated that according to the law on Cambodian political parties, even if Sam Rainsy does not resign, he will lose his position because he is a prisoner.
Mr. Hing Soksan also criticized Mr. Sam Rainsy's decision at that time as a decision that lost the political psychology of Samdech Techo Hun Sen, the Prime Minister of Cambodia and the President of the ruling party.
