The opening ceremony of the technical training course for the rescue team of Cambodia and the technical equipment to support the rescue work was held this morning, June 29, 2020 at the headquarters of the 911 Special Parachute Brigade.
The ceremony was presided over by Senior Minister Kun Kim, First Vice President of the National Committee for Disaster Management, representing Samdech Techo Hun Sen, Prime Minister of Cambodia and Chairman of the National Committee for Disaster Management, and General Ith Sarath, General Deputy Commander and Joint Chief of Staff of the Royal Cambodian Armed Forces.
The opening ceremony of the capacity building training and providing technical equipment to support the operation of the rescue force was also attended by relevant officials from both the National Committee for Disaster Management and the Royal Cambodian Armed Forces.
Senior Minister Kun Kim stated at the opening ceremony of the training course and handed over the equipment that the opening of the training course and equipment of these technical equipment is a consideration of Samdech Techo Hun Sen in strengthening the capacity of the emergency response team. Disasters such as building collapses, natural disasters and accidents on land, water and air.
The Senior Minister added that after Samdech Techo Prime Minister saw the high demand for capacity and skills as well as technical equipment to serve the rescue operation, Samdech also ordered to organize the structure of the rescue team to be clear and additional training on the ability of They.
The rescue squad was made up of about 100 people from six units, including 10 from the 70th Brigade, 50 from the Special Parachute Brigade, and 10 from the National Gendarmerie. 10 Commanding Engineers, 10 1st Brigade and 10 Naval Command.
The training lasts 37 hours and 30 minutes over 5 days with training on the basics of disaster management, disaster management mechanisms and roles and responsibilities, disaster response coordination skills, the basics of Incident control systems, incident estimation, incident control systems, and material resources in operational control systems and incident resource management.
Phay Sok Koma, an adviser to the National Committee for Disaster Management and a trainer, said the rescue team had some experience in responding to rescue operations. However, they still have some shortcomings that need to be improved and supplemented during the rescue operation.
He said that in the past operations, there are still shortcomings, such as understanding of the concept of risk management, the establishment of rescue operations headquarters. Mr. Sok Koma said the training will focus on disaster management policies as well as learning about incident control systems.
"Fighting on the battlefield is one thing. We carry guns, but this is a rescue on the battlefield. It is difficult on the battlefield for bloodshed," he said. "This is a fight to save the people. It is very important. At any time, as long as the people are alive, we must fight."
Therefore, the current training will help strengthen the rescue team in their operation. "I have more hope because the experience has its strengths and weaknesses," he said. Strengths: There are a total of more than ten thousand troops, there is only one combat force. We sincerely hope that you will succeed after studying.
Saving the life of the victim from another accident is the most necessary and important thing for the heart of the rescuer. They do not care how difficult the rescue operation is, their goal is to save the lives of the victims from the dead. This was confirmed by the rescue team who attended the training.
Rescuers see that the training will help strengthen their capacity when launching rescue operations in the event of an accident.
Lieutenant Ing Ratana from the Naval Command has been involved in various disaster relief operations, including the collapse of a building in Kep province. Lt. Col. Ing Ratana said that saving the lives of the people is very important because the lives of the dead can not be resurrected, so the rescue team must work hard to find the victims of other accidents. ⁇.
Lt. Col. Ing Ratana expressed the view that the rescue skills training will make him and other rescue workers more capable of rescuing people from various disasters.
It should be noted that during the opening ceremony of this training course this morning, the Senior Minister also handed over tools and equipment to support the rescue operation to these rescuers.
Equipment and materials to support these rescue operations at a total cost of about 700,000 US dollars donated by Samdech Techo Hun Sen, Prime Minister of Cambodia.
Minister Thong Khon: Cambodia's tourism sector has been severely affected by the Kovid-19 crisis, but some positive signs have been observed since the beginning of May
(Phnom Penh): Mr. Thong Khon, Minister of Tourism, said that although the tourism sector in Cambodia has been severely affected by the crisis of Kovid-19, but observed some positive signs since the beginning of May.
Mr. Thong Khon made this remark during the ceremony to announce the implementation of tourism safety rules and codes of conduct for the six tourism businesses in the country, and then the Ministry of Tourism and the Phnom Penh Capital Administration found 33 tourism businesses. Properly complied with the legal standards this morning, June 29.
Mr. Thong Khon stated that the decrease in the number of tourists has really affected many tour operators. As of May 08, 2020, a total of 2,956 tourism businesses were closed and temporarily closed and staffed. Serves a total of 45,405 people. However, we observe that Phnom Penh is not as severely affected as other major tourist destinations.
This means that the number of businesses closed and suspended due to the Kovid-19 crisis was only 124 locations, equivalent to about 10.43% of the total number of countries, and directly affected 1,960 jobs in the sector, equivalent to 9.33%. Of the total number of tourism staff nationwide (this figure does not include adult tourism services and spa and massage services that have been suspended by the Royal Government).
This loss of employment has had a profound impact on the economy and society as a whole through its impact beyond the negative impact on global tourism and tourism.
He concluded that the domestic tourism movement will recover faster than the return of international tourists as long as travel-related restrictions continue. We have now received information about the intention to reopen transit in some countries, and some countries have already decided to reopen, but have decided to close immediately after the new copy. Recurrence.
