(Phnom Penh): Most people have already encountered on social media, with some media institutions and individuals publishing information in the form of articles, photos and videos to get the attention of the public and the news. Most of that information is baseless.
Other information is false information that is intended to mislead the public into fear of an individual or event, or to indicate that there will be a catastrophic event or incident. Which leads to danger to society, including humanity. These are "fake information" activities that need to be managed in an orderly manner, otherwise it can have a negative impact on society.
To make it clearer today, Fresh New Media, in collaboration with the Cambodian Lawyers for International Cooperation, raised a topic on "Information Fraud" to explain and share it with all citizens and students as follows: :
Within the professional framework of media coverage, all activities need to be in accordance with professional ethics for journalists, as journalism plays an important role in society in presenting information in the form of articles, photographs, audio or video. Video and so on.
All information published must be factual, non-exaggerated and beneficial to society as a whole. If the information is not true, it can clearly affect society as a whole.
As for the Law on the Press, Article 7, paragraphs 3 and 6 also stipulates the ethics of journalists, and journalists must report only on the facts they know from their origins. Journalists must not eliminate important information or falsify other documents. On the other hand, avoid disseminating information that incites racism, color, sex, language, beliefs, religions, opinions or political tendencies, national origin, social status, resources or other circumstances.
Therefore, in the event that a person violates the rules as well as professional principles for the dissemination of information as described above, that person's actions will be considered as "forgery".
In summary, whether individuals, institutions or journalists, the law does not provide the freedom to disseminate information arbitrarily, and any person or institution that commits the above offenses will be punished. According to the Penal Code of the Kingdom of Cambodia.
