The World Bank says the project will help increase security, land tenure and access to infrastructure and agricultural and social services for the landless poor and indigenous communities in Cambodia. Helping smallholder farmers and rural communities access land and natural resources in a sustainable and sustainable way is a core part of the Royal Government of Cambodia's poverty reduction strategy.
The government has established a legal framework for social land concessions that expand land tenure for the landless poor in rural areas. At the same time, the government is promoting indigenous peoples' land tenure to strengthen their land tenure rights.
Since 2008, the LASED Phase 1 and Phase 2 projects, supported by the World Bank, have supported social land concessions in order to promote the distribution of land to the poor through a sustainable and transparent process. Success.
These projects have helped to develop rural roads, build small-scale irrigation systems, build primary schools, health posts, provide technical training, improve cultivation and support the creation of more services for farmers.
Through these two projects, Cambodia has allocated 17,000 hectares of land for housing and cultivation to more than 5,000 landless and landless families, of which more than 3,360 families have received land titles. Already.
"Access to land and the provision of services and training in agricultural techniques to increase agricultural productivity as well as better access to public services are very important," said Ingonado Pracha, World Bank Country Manager for Cambodia. For the poor living in rural areas.
Granting land tenure for indigenous communities will also support the ongoing land tenure process of indigenous communities.
At the same time, the project will provide assistance to communities that have completed the process of granting land tenure to indigenous communities. The LASED Phase 3 project will cover 71 locations and communities in all provinces of Cambodia except Phnom Penh.
The project will support the construction of rural roads, micro-irrigation systems, water supply and sanitation facilities, school buildings, teachers' houses, health posts and community centers. New beneficiaries of the social land concession program will receive assistance for their first year of cultivation, including seeds.
The project will also provide technical assistance to implement climate-wise agricultural techniques, establish farmers' associations for production and marketing activities, and to manage community funds for local economic expansion. U.S. astronomers likely spotted two combined black holes three million times brighter than the sun
Astronomers have also calculated that such a large amount of light can be formed when two large black holes combine to form a superficial mass of dust and hot gas. The light shines unparalleled in the boundless space, and it is certain that the Earth's telescope is capable of perceiving that amazing phenomenon.
