This morning, Samdech Techo Hun Sen attends the 36th ASEAN Summit under the theme "Cohesion and Response"
(Phnom Penh): On June 26, 2020, Samdech Techo Hun Sen, Prime Minister of Cambodia, attended the 36th ASEAN Summit under the theme "Cohesion and Response" via Video at the Peace Palace. .
The meeting was held at the invitation of Vietnamese Prime Minister Nguyen Tan Dung. According to a press release from the Ministry of Foreign Affairs and International Cooperation, the 36th ASEAN Summit will focus on ASEAN's joint efforts to tackle the epidemic. Global Kovid-19 and the promotion of post-Kovid-19 socio-economic recovery.
The meeting will also discuss ways and means to achieve the ASEAN Community Vision 2025 and ASEAN's relations with external partners and the global community.
In a press release, the Prime Minister will also attend a number of meetings, including a special meeting of ASEAN leaders on the occasion of the 36th ASEAN Summit on the Promotion of the Role of Women in the Digital Age. , ASEAN Leaders Meeting with ASEAN Inter-Parliamentary Representatives, ASEAN Leaders Meeting with ASEAN Youth Representatives and ASEAN Leaders Meeting with Representatives of the ASEAN Business Advisory Council.
The Ministry of Foreign Affairs said that the Prime Minister was accompanied by Deputy Prime Minister Prak Sokhon, Minister of Foreign Affairs and International Cooperation, Mr. Pan Sosak, Minister of Commerce, and Mr. Hang Chuon Naron, Minister of Education and Youth. And Sports, Mr. Mam Bunheng, Minister of Health and Ms. Ing Kantha Phavi, Minister of Women's Affairs, as well as some senior officials of the Royal Government of Cambodia.
The Association of Southeast Asian Nations was established on August 8, 1967. Initially, there were five countries: Malaysia, Indonesia, the Philippines, Singapore and Thailand. The founding countries, with the exception of Thailand, were all newly occupied territories from the yoke of colonialism at the time.
Coupled with the onset of the Cold War, the five countries are urged to form an association with the common goal of promoting peace, stability, economic growth, as well as social and cultural development in the whole of Southeast Asia. Since 1984, five other countries have joined. The five countries are: Brunei, Vietnam, Laos, Myanmar and Cambodia (April 30, 1999).
The ASEAN Summit is an important and necessary annual event in the region. The meeting is usually held annually in the host countries of the meeting. Leaders of member countries often gather at the event, but for 2020, due to the 19-year-old disease, all leaders will have to meet on video instead.
It should be noted that before the ASEAN Summit on June 24, 2020, Deputy Prime Minister Prak Sokhon, Minister of Foreign Affairs and International Cooperation, also attended the Informal Meeting of ASEAN Foreign Ministers. The 21st Political-Security Community Council and the 26th ASEAN Coordinating Council Meeting via video.
At the informal meeting, the ASEAN Foreign Ministers noted the growing interest from external partners in linking with ASEAN, the Foreign Ministry said. The ASEAN Foreign Ministers also exchanged views on regional and international issues, such as the situation on the Korean Peninsula, Rahang State and developments in the South China Sea.
The ASEAN Foreign Ministers shared the views on the importance of strengthening the ASEAN Central Committee in the development of regional architecture, strengthening regional peace and stability, especially the acceleration of work to link closer cooperation in ASEAN in response to the recovery situation from Kovid-19 disease.
Separately, Deputy Prime Minister Prak Sokhon also shared his views. Mr. Prak Sokhon said that in addition to the pandemic and socio-economic challenges of ASEAN, ASEAN is also facing geopolitical difficulties due to the increasing strategic competition of the superpowers.
The Deputy Prime Minister said that despite the pressure on ASEAN, the ASEAN member states are not interested in electing a superpower and continue to support a multilateral system based on the rule of law.
As for the situation in Ra Khanh, Cambodia encourages the continuation of dialogue between stakeholders to ensure safe, orderly and dignified repatriation.
On the Korean Peninsula issue, Cambodia emphasized the importance of ASEAN's role, especially through the ASEAN Regional Forum mechanism and other confidence-building measures to promote positive development on the Korean Peninsula. Cambodia appreciates the joint initiatives of Russia, the United States, China and Japan in developing an action plan.
On the issue of the South China Sea, Cambodia, as a non-sovereign state in the South China Sea, maintains neutrality on territorial disputes, and Cambodia hopes that the parties concerned should continue to maintain a favorable environment for maintaining peace, security and stability in the South China Sea. Areas that will contribute to the conclusion of code of conduct negotiations in the South China Sea.
For the 21st meeting of the ASEAN Political-Security Community (APSC), the meeting noted the progress of the implementation of the detailed plan of the ASEAN Security-Political Community, in which 96% of the above activities were implemented. The meeting looked forward to the completion of the mid-term review report of the ASEAN Community Vision 2025, which includes a detailed plan of the ASEAN Political-Security Community 2025.
The Deputy Prime Minister also said that since 2017-2019, Cambodia, the host country of the Center, has contributed US $ 675,000 to ensure the sustainability of the Center's operations and promote ASEAN's adequate contribution to Ensure the sustainability of the center operation.
