US provides $ 56 million in grant funding to support Cambodia's four sectors
(Phnom Penh): The Royal Government of Cambodia, through the Council for the Development of Cambodia and the United States Government through the United States Agency for International Development (USAID), on June 25, 2020, signed an amendment to the Development Cooperation Fund for 2019.
Under the agreement, the US government will provide about $ 56 million in grant funding, including $ 38 million for health and education. And $ 18 million for agriculture and the environment, implemented by project implementation agencies in Cambodia.
According to Mr. Chin Bun Sean, Senior Minister in charge of Special Missions and Vice Chairman of the Council for the Development of Cambodia, "Continuing this bilateral development cooperation is an important pillar in promoting and strengthening cooperation and partnership." "Both."
Through cooperation with the Ministry of Health, this support will continue to contribute to reducing maternal and infant mortality under the age of five, as well as supporting programs to reduce stunting and anemia in children and Women and reduce the prevalence of AIDS, tuberculosis and malaria in Cambodia.
At the same time, in cooperation with the Ministry of Environment, this agreement will also support environmental programs to reduce the vulnerability of people as well as landscapes affected by climate change and help Cambodia achieve its commitment to Its international framework for reducing greenhouse gas emissions, especially those caused by deforestation and degradation.
Since March 2016, including the agreement signed today, the US side has provided a total of $ 253 million in grant funding to support the four priority areas. This support responds well to the priorities of the Royal Government of Cambodia.
In addition, the United States Government provides support to civil society organizations that focus on human rights, anti-trafficking, and accountability and transparency in Cambodia. These efforts complement our work with the Royal Government in supporting sustainable and environmental development in Cambodia.
