(Siem Reap): Seven out of 18 walls of Angkor Thom city damaged by natural disasters, atmosphere, stone age, dilapidated structures and termites have been repaired by experts of APSARA Authority in 135 meters in three phases. Different using the entire national budget.
The renovation was done in order to preserve and strengthen the walls of Angkor Thom to maintain the beauty of the walls and gates of Angkor Thom to last a long time. The renovation process started in 2014 with a total length of 135 meters, of which 20 meters east of the pottery gate and 115 meters west of the Om River gate.
Mao Sokny, director of the Angkor Thom city wall repair site and an official of the Department of Temple Preservation in Angkor Park and Preventive Archeology, said that the APSARA Authority had selected high-risk areas to repair first because those sites were severely affected. By nature.
In the repair work, the team focused on the technique of preparing laterite walls from one layer to another, compacting the soil to strengthen the walls to be highly secure, prevent water infiltration, improve the quality of the aesthetic construction by maintaining the original form of Walls and research the history of past repairs.
Not only that, APSARA Authority also considers the safety of the construction to maintain the sustainability by sloping the river and planting grass along the foot to prevent erosion.
For the repair of Angkor Thom wall, it takes a lot of time because it needs to study the structure, construction, repair history and high risk points. In particular, when repairing, it is required to comply with the Angkor Charter, to monitor the work and evaluation of the experts of the CC-Angkor Commission, including the maintenance of resistance, pruning branches and removing plants that grow on the walls.
For future plans, APSARA Authority plans to renovate the walls of Angkor Thom, which suffered all the dangers, through safe conservation step by step to increase the value of the ancestral heritage in the long run. Sar Sokha Scholarship Chair brings donations to two homeless old women living in Phsar Doeum Thkov commune
